<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><DIV class=postcolor>By Donna Freydkin, <STRONG>USA TODAY<BR></STRONG>NEW YORK —<B> Madonna</B> was as sweet as a lamb when she arrived at <B>Solo, Manhattan's new Mediterranean kosher eatery</B>, after her concert <B>last Thursday.</B><BR>The star, who was there to mark the 31st birthday of Kabbalah Rabbi Michael Berg, dined on Solo's upscale cuisine, including lamb chops and glazed duck breast. <BR><BR>The restaurant is the brainchild of owner <B>Joey Allaham</B>, who <B>opened Solo on June 10 in the atrium of the Sony building</B> and prides himself on its earthy decor and quiet elegance. Mayor Michael Bloomberg has been there. <BR><BR>As for <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>, she was part of a group of 20 guests, <STRONG>including designer Donna Karan</STRONG>, eating in one of the restaurant's private rooms. She sat next to her husband, <STRONG>Guy Ritchie</STRONG>, the entire night and, according to the Solo's rep, was low-maintenance and laid-back. <BR><BR>But, as always, it pays to be the <STRONG>world's best-known songstress, who showed up around 11:30 p.m</STRONG>. with several bodyguards in tow and stayed <STRONG>until 3 a.m</STRONG>. When Madonna ordered lamb and was told that the restaurant had run out, one of her fellow diners gave her their dish.<BR><BR><STRONG>Madonna also has frequently dined at Allaham's Manhattan Kosher steakhouse, Prime Grill. <BR><BR></STRONG>"I'm able to accommodate her and keep her happy, because she expects a lot," he says. "I make sure that everything she eats is the best she's ever had." <BR><BR>Assuming he hasn't run out of it, that is. <BR><BR>About the restaurant: Solo, 550 Madison Ave., 212-833-7800 <BR></DIV><BR>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


